\import{base-macros}
\author{kellenkanarios}
\title{Directory protocols expose transient states because transactions span multiple hops}
\tag{public}

\p{Because a [directory](KIT8) transaction takes several hops (requestor → home → owner → …), a line spends observable time in \em{transient states} between the stable ones — e.g. #{\mathrm{MI}^{A}} ("modified, going to invalid, waiting on an acknowledgment"). These are not an implementation detail to wave away: concurrent transactions to the same line race, and the protocol must define a correct outcome for every interleaving. That combinatorial state space is what makes directory protocols hard to get right.}

\example{L holds a dirty copy and starts a writeback to home H. Concurrently R sends a read to H. R sits in #{\mathrm{S}^{D}} ("shared, waiting for data") until the race resolves.
\figure{
\<html:img>[width]{100\%}[src]{\route-asset{assets/img/example-race.png}}{}
}
}

\remark{Never resolve these races with NACKs — they cause starvation. The [[HHZO]] Murphi implementation works through the channel-separation arguments that keep the transient-state space deadlock-free.}
